editorial:
## Method and reasoning
Sort the stalls. To test gap d, start at the leftmost stall and always take the first stall at least d beyond the last selected one. By induction, the ith greedy placement is no farther right than the ith placement of any feasible layout. Feasibility decreases as d increases, so binary-search the last feasible d.

## Walk through the sample
With gap 3 the sorted stalls allow 1, 4, 8. With gap 4 the earliest choices are 1, 8, after which even stall 9 is too close. Failure of the earliest placement rules out every later placement.

## Complexity
Sorting costs O(n log n). With coordinate span D, the feasibility scans take O(n log(D+2)). Store O(n) coordinates.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n, k;
    cin >> n >> k;
    vector<long long> a(n);
    for (auto &x : a)
        cin >> x;
    sort(a.begin(), a.end());
    long long lo = 0, hi = a.back() - a.front();
    while (lo < hi) {
        long long mid = lo + (hi - lo + 1) / 2, last = a.front();
        int count = 1;
        for (long long x : a)
            if (x - last >= mid) {
                last = x;
                ++count;
            }
        if (count >= k)
            lo = mid;
        else
            hi = mid - 1;
    }
    cout << lo << '\n';
}
~~~
